Native IEEE 802.11bn UHR decoding
================================

This branch adds native Wi-Fi 8 decoding to Ben Greear's wireshark-ct
4.7.0 tree, based on df3e59be7f72b233568d53ea8583a109a89c4764.
Wireshark and TShark share these fields; the Lua postdissector is not needed.

Implemented
-----------

* UHR Operation, extension 151: control flags and optional DPS, NPCA,
  P-EDCA and DBE parameters, including disabled subchannel bitmaps.
* UHR Capabilities, extension 152: fixed six-octet MAC and five-octet PHY
  capabilities, public bit definitions, and AP-only DBE capability tails.
* SMD Information, extension 154: ID, capabilities and timeout.
* UHR Mode Change, extension 157: tuple controls, mode IDs and raw mode data.
* Protected UHR category 43: Request/Response/Notify action codes, dialog
  token, type and Response Count. Variable action bodies remain raw.
* UHR/EHT Supported Rates membership selectors (0xf8/0xf9).
* Names for extensions 153, 155, 156, 158 and 159; their payloads remain
  undecoded. Existing native UHR Trigger decoding is retained unchanged.

This is partial native support, not a complete 802.11bn implementation or
conformance checker. DUO internal bits, DBE EHT MCS/NSS map contents,
mode specific data, action variable bodies and unsupported extra bytes
remain raw. No UHR PPDU/radiotap metadata or vendor formats are invented.
Protected action bodies must already be decrypted using Wireshark's usual
mechanism. Hardware/drivers must still deliver the frames and metadata.

Choosing the reference layout
-----------------------------

Preferences > Protocols > IEEE 802.11 > UHR reference layout:

    d1_5       default, pinned public D1.5 reference
    pre_d1_5   older pinned Linux reference 410d70acf9ca

Or on the command line:

    build-uhr/run/tshark -o wlan.uhr_layout:d1_5 -r capture.pcapng
    build-uhr/run/tshark -o wlan.uhr_layout:pre_d1_5 -r capture.pcapng

The generated wlan.uhr.layout field identifies the chosen interpretation.
The packet does not identify its draft. The decoder does not guess a draft
from length, and pre_d1_5 does not cover every earlier/vendor revision.
Confirm the Qualcomm firmware's reference before drawing conclusions.

D1.5 removed the four-byte Basic UHR MCS/NSS Set from Operation. Therefore,
an Operation containing only two control bytes can be complete under that
reference. The previous Lua decoder's missing-four-bytes warning is not
proof of a firmware defect. D1.5 also moved presence bits, changed NPCA
Primary Channel from an offset to a channel number, changed P-EDCA packing,
and changed parts of MAC and DBE Capability packing.

Presence bits locate optional fields independently of feature enable bits.
D1.5 Linux walkers skip one raw DUO octet. For pre-D1.5 DUO or unassigned
Operation control bits, later offsets are deliberately not assumed.
AP DBE tails are decoded in Beacon, Probe Response, Association Response
and Reassociation Response elements. Non-AP DBE support does not imply an
AP-only tail. Disable the old wifi8_uhr.lua postdissector to avoid two views
with different draft assumptions and filter names.

Useful display filters
----------------------

    wlan.ext_tag.number == 151
    wlan.ext_tag.number == 152
    wlan.uhr.operation.npca_ena == 1
    wlan.uhr.operation.npca_pres == 1
    wlan.uhr.npca.primary_chan == 44
    wlan.uhr.npca.primary_channel_offset
    wlan.uhr.capabilities.mac.npca_supp == 1
    wlan.uhr.mode_change.mode_id == 1
    wlan.uhr.action
    wlan.uhr.truncated
    wlan.uhr.undecoded_data

Numeric fields described as codes retain the encoded value; do not assume
that every timing or bandwidth code is a literal number of us or MHz.

Build on Fedora / LANforge
-------------------------

    bash tools/build-uhr-fedora.sh --install-deps
    ./build-uhr/run/wireshark

Without installing dependencies:

    bash tools/build-uhr-fedora.sh

The helper uses the tree's RPM setup script for dependencies, explicitly
maps CMake to /usr/bin/gcc and /usr/bin/g++, builds the Qt GUI and TShark,
and runs the UHR regression suite. It uses /usr/bin/python3 by default to
avoid unintentionally selecting Python from an active virtualenv.
It prints compiler paths and versions. No global compiler symlinks change,
and the system Wireshark installation is not replaced.

Custom toolchain or Python:

    CC=/path/to/gcc CXX=/path/to/g++ UHR_PYTHON=/path/to/python3 \
        UHR_BUILD_DIR="$PWD/build-custom" UHR_BUILD_JOBS=2 \
        bash tools/build-uhr-fedora.sh

CC/CXX must name executable files, without flags or wrapper arguments.
Python must have pytest. A cache with different compilers is rejected;
choose a fresh build directory when changing toolchains. An equivalent
cached compiler spelling is reused so CMake does not reset other settings.
CMake selects the language standard required by this tree and Qt.
UHR_BUILD_TYPE can select Release instead of default RelWithDebInfo.

Fedora 39 may require archived dependency repositories. A successful build
on Debian does not establish Fedora 39 runtime compatibility. An AppImage
is a separate packaging task; this branch provides source and a build helper.

Validation
----------

Verified on Debian 13 x86_64 with GCC/G++ 14.2.0, CMake 3.31.6 and Qt 6.8.2:
the Qt Wireshark GUI and TShark build with warnings treated as errors,
Wireshark's version command runs, and all 49 synthetic regression cases
pass (including 512 deterministic mutated frames across the two layouts).
The field registry contains 142 UHR fields plus two expert fields and
reports no registration errors. Compiler mismatch and missing compiler
checks were also exercised. This verification used Release mode, Lua off
and live-capture helpers off; it does not test a Fedora installation,
capture permissions, every optional dependency or a GUI interaction session.

    python3 -m pytest test/suite_uhr.py --program-path=/path/to/build/run \
        --disable-capture --disable-gui -o addopts= -q

The tests generate synthetic frames in temporary files. Coverage includes
both reference layouts, enable/presence distinction, optional alignment,
AP/non-AP tails, six management subtypes, SMD, Mode Change tuples, action
headers, truncation, repeated elements, IE boundaries and deterministic
mutations. No private captures, vendor identifiers or credentials are
included. Customer captures should stay outside the repository.

Public sources
--------------

D1.5 structures, masks, and NPCA/DBE walkers (pinned):
https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/067c5c24cef23d913b67eaaf71ae67e7169a6620/include/linux/ieee80211-uhr.h
SHA256 of that header:
78f281c3b4e46c37e7399013d9e6d1b15999eb533b6a4dcf9018e2950e90d2e5

Pre-D1.5 layout:
https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/410d70acf9ca73fdf370e1eb7e1da64e486dcbb5/include/linux/ieee80211-uhr.h

Extension IDs, membership selectors and action headers at the current pin:
https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/067c5c24cef23d913b67eaaf71ae67e7169a6620/include/linux/ieee80211.h

Public hostapd D1.5 change discussion:
https://lists.infradead.org/pipermail/hostap/2026-July/045392.html

Existing upstream UHR Trigger support:
https://gitlab.com/wireshark/wireshark/-/merge_requests/20410

No NDA Windows dissector, private source or unpublished IEEE draft text was
used. The public references are draft snapshots, not a final specification.
